// Book One, Chapter 12 (session 12). The first night of the hunt: three places out of four, and what the reckoning is going to be.
// One night phase of three actions. Ends with rest().
@chapter b1c12
@title What the Lamp Remembers
@book burning-city
@version 1

# start
## The first night of the hunt
$ do phase("night", 3)
$ b1.farm = "unmet"
$ b1.clerk = "unmet"
$ b1.boy = "unmet"
$ b1.scribe = "none"
$ b1.reckoning = "mixed"
$ unflag b1.ledger
$ unflag b1.hill_known
$ _spared = 0
$ _slain = 0
$ _hunters = 0
$ do discover("the_igniferi")
The hunt begins at dusk, as it was said that it would, with no horn and no word. There is nothing in the streets to show it. The city, under its pall of ash, goes about the business of burying itself, and the covens of Rome go out through it in ones and twos, quite silently, with their hoods up, like people who have remembered an appointment.
?if b1.cell == "house"
  The house hunts as it hunts everything, from the ledger outward. Hanno has the first of it laid out on the table in the counting room, a list of forty names in his small black hand, with a line drawn under four of them. Prisca does not come down. She has sent, instead, a very short note, which says that you will know what to do, and that it would be better if you were quiet about it.
?elif b1.cell == "theron"
  The General's line makes its camp, as it makes everything, in a tidy square, in the dark behind the Capena gate, in a ruined temple with the roof gone, and there is a smell of oiled leather and of men who have been a long time in the field. The captain with the scarred jaw gives you a cloak, a short blade, and nothing at all by way of welcome. She says that she is called a name that you will not be able to say, and that you may call her Captain. She says that a cell of hers has found a farm.
?elif b1.cell == "meritamun"
  The Egyptian quarter does not hunt. It reads. You come to a door in a street of tanners, at the second hour, as you were told, and are taken down a stair by a silent girl with a lamp that is not lit, into a long low room full of the smell of cedar and old papyrus, where Meritamun is sitting cross legged on a chest in the middle of a hundred scrolls. She says that it is a very good thing that you have come, and that it is a pity about the lamp. She says that she has had the great misfortune to find out where a number of things are.
?else
  You go alone. It is both the simplest and the most dangerous thing that you can do, and you find, at the cellar grate, in the last of the light, that you are perfectly calm. There is a lamp left burning on the sill, as Prisca said. It is very small. You put it out with your fingers, and the smell of the wick goes with you for a long way.
?end
?if b1.war_terms == "total"
  It is Theron's war, and everyone knows it, and the streets feel it the way a stable feels a fire in the next yard. The word is that nothing is to be left standing. It is said quietly, in many places at once, and it has already begun to be believed.
?elif b1.war_terms == "mercy"
  It is a war with a rule in it. You know the rule, because you made it, and you are aware, as the dusk comes down, of how very much it is going to depend on the people who have to keep it.
?else
  It is a war with a limit, and every cell in the city has been told where the limit is, and every cell has its own idea of how far it extends.
?end
There are four places that you could go, and a night in which to go to three. It is a good deal less than the whole of a city.
-> hub

# hub
## The night
:hub
?if actions_left() == 3
  It is early. The darkness has a clean look, and a number of the shutters in the Velabrum have not yet been put up, and you can still hear the market in the next street counting its dead.
?elif actions_left() == 2
  It is the middle of the night. The ash has stopped falling, and the stars, in the gaps between the smoke, have the dull wet look of coins that have been in a river.
?else
  It is very late. The light in the east is not yet anything but a thought, and you have a good deal still to carry, and the night is getting short.
?end
* Go to the lamp seller's shop in the Velabrum [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> shop
  $ do act()
* Go out the Appian Way, where a cell has found a farm [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> farm
  $ do act()
* Go to the Aventine station, where Rufus is holding a man [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> station
  $ do act()
* Go to the Egyptian quarter, to the Scribe line's rooms [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> scribe
  $ do act()
?if actions_left() <= 0
  $ do end_phase()
  -> dawn
?end
-> :hub

# shop
## The lamp seller's shop
The shop is at the bottom of a narrow lane in the Velabrum, between a cheesemonger and a shuttered temple of a god that nobody has worshipped since the Republic. It is shut. The sign over the door is a clay lamp, painted white, with a small neat flame. The yard behind it, where they found the six men, has been swept, by someone who knew no one would come to look at what was left, and there is a dark stain in the corner that is not the colour of rust.
?if b1.flavus == "struck"
  You have been here before, by day. You remember a gentle lame man with kind eyes, who sold you a lamp, and said that it would be a very bright summer, and whom you struck across the face. He promised to remember yours. It is not a thing that you think of with much pride, and it is not a thing that you are able to forget.
?elif b1.flavus == "spoke"
  You have been here before, by day. You remember a gentle lame man with kind eyes, who talked to you for a quarter of an hour about the nature of fire, as a man talks about a friend, and who sold you a lamp. The lamp is still in a cupboard in the house. You have not been able to bring yourself to light it.
?else
  You have not been in this lane before. It is a clean, narrow, respectable place, and it smells of lamp oil and mice. It is hard to believe that anything has ever been decided here.
?end
There is somebody in the loft. You hear him before you reach the stair. It is a small, quick, terrified heartbeat, as of a creature in a wall, and the sound of a reed on papyrus, scratching very fast.
He is a thin stooped man of fifty in a clerk's tunic, with ink on his fingers and a cloth tied round his head, and he has made himself a nest of scrolls in the angle of the roof. He has been there for three days. He has a bottle of water and the end of a loaf. He looks up as you come through the trap, and he does not scream. He has the extraordinary face of a man who has been expecting you for so long that it is a relief.
"Ah," says the clerk. "It is you. I had thought that it would be somebody older."
He holds up his hands. They are shaking. They are full of ink.
"My name is Decimus," he says. "I kept the book. I want you to understand that I did not light anything. I do not hold with it. I have said so to the master, many times, in the proper manner, and he has said that I was a very useful man, and that it was a blessing that the work had need of one who did not." He swallows. "It is the book that you want. It has every jar, and every wick, and every coin, and every name. I have been copying it. I thought, if I did not stop, that I should not have to think about the thing at the end."
?if b1.cell == "theron" or b1.war_terms == "total"
  There is a sound on the stair. It is a very light, very careful one, such as is made by two people who have been taught to climb in the dark, and you know the step. You have heard it all evening, in the camp. It is the General's young hunters, the two that came into the temple at dusk, bright eyed, fresh turned, who did not wait to be told.
  They come up through the trap together. They are twenty or so, in their way, and eager, and one of them has a knife. They look at the clerk, and then at you, and they smile, a little shyly, as dogs smile at a person who has been set to keep an eye on them.
  "The captain said that you would be here," says the elder of them. "It is quite all right. We will see to it."
  $ _hunters = 1
?end
He has the book on his knees, in a satchel, with both hands on the strap.
:the_clerk
?if _hunters > 0
  * Stand between them and the clerk, and tell them that you are taking him
    -> shop_fight
  * Step aside, and let them do what they came to do
    $ b1.clerk = "dead"
    $ _slain += 1
    $ humanity -= 3
    $ standing += 1
    $ flag b1.ledger
    You step aside. It is a plain thing, and a very small one, a foot and a half of floor. Decimus looks at you. He does not say anything. He has the face of a man who has just been told the answer to a sum that he already knew, and it is a very quiet moment, and it is over very quickly. The elder hunter hands you the book with a little bow. It is a good book. It is exactly as neat as he said.
    -> clerk_end
?else
  * Take him, and the book, and bring them to Meritamun
    ~> clerk_kept
    -> clerk_end
  * Take the book, and put him on the road with silver in his hand
    ~> clerk_freed
    -> clerk_end
  * Take the book, and do what the war says
    $ b1.clerk = "dead"
    $ _slain += 1
    $ humanity -= 4
    $ do kill("human")
    $ cunning_brutal += 2
    $ flag b1.ledger
    He does not try to run. That is the thing that you keep. He sits with his hands in his lap and the ink on his fingers and he looks, with great attention, at the wall, and he says, as you come across the loft, in a small hoarse voice, that it was a very good book. It is over in a moment. The loft is very quiet afterward, with the lamplight on the scattered scrolls. You pick up the satchel. It is warm.
    -> clerk_end
?end
-> clerk_end

# shop_fight
$ do fight("fledgling", 1)
:exchange
* Put him behind you and meet them, hard
  $ do strike("brutal")
* Make it a matter of the captain's own word
  $ do strike("talk")
* Let the blood loose ?if not must_feed()
  $ do strike("power")
?if not fight_over()
  ?if last_strike() == "hit"
    The elder of them goes back against the wall with a grunt, and the knife rattles on the boards.
  ?else
    The younger one is quicker than he looks, and you feel the edge across your forearm.
  ?end
  -> :exchange
?end
?if fight_result() == "won" or fight_result() == "talked"
  $ standing -= 1
  $ bond.theron -= 1
  They go. They do not say a word. They go down the stair together with the exact, offended dignity of the very young, and you know that they will tell the captain, and that the captain will not be pleased. It is something that you will have to pay for. You have bought a man's life, and you have, quite precisely, no idea yet what it was worth.
  -> clerk_his
?else
  $ injury += 6
  They get the clerk. It is done quickly, in the corner of the loft, with a small wet sound, and a scatter of papyrus that goes up like a flock of birds. The book falls out of the satchel. You are on one knee against the wall, with your arm open, and one of them, quite kindly, picks the book up and offers it to you. He is sorry about the mess. It is the cruellest thing that has been said to you tonight.
  $ b1.clerk = "dead"
  $ _slain += 1
  $ flag b1.ledger
  -> clerk_end
?end

# clerk_his
## The clerk
:clerk_his
?if b1.war_terms == "total"
  There is nowhere in a war like this to take a man who has done nothing. You say so to yourself, as you lead him down the stair, and it is not a comforting thing to hear.
?end
* Take him, and the book, and bring them to Meritamun
  ~> clerk_kept
  -> clerk_end
* Take the book, and put him on the road with silver in his hand
  ~> clerk_freed
  -> clerk_end

# clerk_kept
$ b1.clerk = "kept"
$ flag b1.ledger
$ flag b1.hill_known
$ _spared += 1
$ humanity += 2
$ velati_regard += 2
"You are quite sure?" says Decimus. He says it with great politeness. He is looking at you as if you were a very odd, very rare animal. "I should like to point out that I am of the sect." He does not seem able to leave it alone. "I should like to say that I do not at all know what to do with myself, now, and that I am very much obliged." He takes his satchel. He puts his hand on the stair rail. He stops. "I should tell you," he says, "since I am in your hands. The master has gone north. Flavus. He left three nights ago, by the Via Salaria, with the best of them. He did not say where. But I wrote the book, and I know what he bought for the journey, and there is a good deal of pitch in it, and a thing that he said once, in the shop, when he thought that I was not listening, which was that the first fire on the hill had not finished. He said that the one that they were looking for was not burned, and that the hill is a house that has been left with its door open." He closes his mouth. He looks at the floor. "I am very sorry," he says. "I think that it is your hill."
It sits in you like a stone dropped into a very deep well. You hear it go all the way down.
<~

# clerk_freed
$ b1.clerk = "freed"
$ flag b1.ledger
$ flag b1.hill_known
$ _spared += 1
$ humanity += 1
$ defiance += 1
You give him twenty denarii and the name of a woman in Ostia who will ask no questions. He holds the coins as though they were hot. "It is not that I do not want to be taken," he says, "I do very much. It is only that I think that I should be of no use." He draws a breath, and he says what he has to say very fast, so as to be rid of it. "The master has gone north. Flavus. Three nights ago, by the Via Salaria, with the best of them. I wrote what he bought. There is a good deal of pitch. He said once that the first fire on the hill had not finished, and that the one they were looking for was not burned." He is shaking. "I should not like to be on the Via Salaria. I think that it is yours, the hill." He goes down the stair, a thin stooped figure, with the cloth round his head, and he does not look back, and you hear his steps in the lane for a long time, going very quickly, and then not.
<~

# clerk_end
## The book
:clerk_end
The book is a roll of papyrus, as thick as your arm, closely written, in a small clear, tireless hand, and it is exactly what he said that it was. It has a column for the jars and a column for the wicks and a column for the pitch, and the pitch is the thing. It is a very long column. It goes back to the spring of the year before, and every line of it has a date, and a quantity, and the name of a seller, and a sum, and in the right hand margin, a small neat letter that is not the name of anyone you know.
There are four letters, repeated. You read them over and over, in the dark. They are an *M*, and a *T*, and a *P*, and an *L*, and the sums against them are very large.
$ do discover("the_igniferi")
?if b1.clerk != "dead"
  You turn to the last pages of the book. They are in a different ink. They are the clerk's own, and they are not about jars. They are a list of names, a long one, of the people who gave money to the sect, in small amounts, from a great many houses, who had thought that they were paying for a hearth.
?end
You roll it up. It weighs, you think, rather less than a man.
<~

# farm
## The farm on the Appian Way
It is beyond the third milestone, in a fold of the land, a long low steading of the old kind, whitewashed, with a row of umbrella pines along the road, and an orchard, and a pen with a number of sleeping sheep. There is a light in the barn. It is a small, steady, yellow one, the colour of a lamp that does not go out, and you can see it for a good half mile, from the road, across the black fields. It is the loveliest thing that you have seen in a week.
There are eight of the General's line in the pine shadow, with their hoods up, and the captain with the scarred jaw is at the head of them. She has been there for an hour, and she has the look of a woman who has been told to wait and has not liked it.
?if b1.cell == "theron"
  She nods, shortly, as you come up. "Good," she says. "I did not think you would come." It is not a compliment. It is a note in a book.
?else
  She looks at you, and at your hood, and she says, in a voice entirely without warmth, "I was not told to expect a visitor." She does not tell you to go. It is the custom, of the hunt.
?end
"There are thirty in the barn," she says. "I have counted heartbeats. Eleven of them are men, and have jars. The rest are women, and the old, and some who are small." She does not say it with any feeling at all. She says it as a woman says the number of a legion's wagons. "They have been there four days. They are waiting for the master. They are praying, I think. I can hear it. It is a kind of low talking."
She looks at you.
?if b1.war_terms == "total"
  "The council's word was all," she says. "You were there. I do not like it more than you do. I will say it plain: I have a son's age in that barn, and I have seen what is done with a thing that is left alone. I do not intend to leave it." She does not draw her blade. "There is a door at the back. It is yours if you want it."
?elif b1.war_terms == "mercy"
  "The council's word was that those who yield may live," she says. "I have kept to it. I have kept to it for the whole of the night, on the road, and I should like it noted that it is the first time in four hundred years that I have been asked to." She turns her hard face to the light in the barn. "They will not yield. I have heard them. It is that kind of praying. But you made the rule, and it is for you to ask them."
?else
  "The council's word was the ones who carried the jars," she says. "I do not know which of them are the ones. I have been trying to decide for an hour. I will say that a man who has put his hand to a jar is a man who has put his hand to a jar, and that I do not at all know what to do about the rest." She looks at you. "You were on the hill. It is for you to say where the line is."
?end
The pines move overhead, and a long way off a dog barks, and it is a perfectly ordinary night in the country, with the smell of sheep and wood smoke and a barn full of hay.
:the_barn
* Go in with the hunters, and finish it
  $ b1.farm = "joined"
  $ _slain += 6
  $ humanity -= 5
  $ cunning_brutal += 4
  $ standing += 1
  $ bond.theron += 1
  $ do feed("human_kill")
  You do not tell this part. You tell it to nobody, then or afterward, not to Prisca, not to the dark, and you find that it will not stay in the order that it happened in. There is the door. There is the light, the white still flame in the jars, that you can feel across a room on your skin like a hand on a hot stove. There is a woman at the door with a ladle. There is the low talking, that does not stop, even when it ought to, and then does. There is the smell of hay. You are very fast, and very strong, and entirely without any feeling, as if you were a thing that had been wound up and set down, and when it is done you are standing in the orchard under the pines with blood to the elbow, and your hunger, that has been with you for a week, is gone. It is the worst thing that has ever happened to you, and you are not sorry for it, and you do not know which of those is the more terrible.
  The captain does not look at you for a long time. When she does, there is something in her face that is not approval, and is not contempt. It is respect, of a kind that one gives to a thing that has been very thoroughly used.
* Sort them at the door: the jars first, and then the rest
  ?if b1.war_terms == "total"
    "It is not what the council said," says the captain. She says it flatly, without heat. She looks at the barn, and then at the sky, and at the long dark rim of the world, and something seems to go out of her shoulders. "I will give you ten minutes," she says. "It is what I have." She does not say that it is a mercy. She says, "Make it count."
  ?end
  ~> farm_sort
  -> farm_end
* Go round to the back door, open it, and tell them to run
  $ b1.farm = "stopped"
  $ standing -= 3
  $ defiance += 3
  $ secrecy -= 1
  ~> farm_open
  -> farm_end
* Stand aside, and do nothing
  $ b1.farm = "stood_aside"
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ defiance -= 1
  ?if b1.war_terms == "total"
    $ _slain += 6
    You stand under the pines and you do not move. You are not required to. It is, in the oldest sense of the phrase, nothing to do with you. You hear it. You have very good ears. You hear the door, and the low talking, and the light going out, one by one, in a sequence like somebody putting out a row of lamps, and you stand quite still with your hands at your sides and your eyes on the road. The captain comes back, afterward, with a cut across the back of her hand. She does not say anything. She sits down on the verge, and takes a long breath, and she looks, for the first time, very old.
  ?elif b1.war_terms == "mercy"
    $ _slain += 2
    $ _spared += 24
    They keep to the rule, because the captain keeps to it. She goes in with two of hers, to the door, and asks, as she was told to, in a flat parade ground Latin that the old and the small do not follow, whether anyone wishes to yield. A good many do. They come out of the barn in a long grey file, with their hands open, with their eyes on the ground, and the old men lean on the women, and the small ones are carried. Seven of the men with jars do not. You do not watch what is done about it. You stand under the pines with your hands at your sides, and you are not at all certain that it was not a way of watching.
  ?else
    $ _slain += 4
    $ _spared += 18
    They do it as they decide it, and it is not well decided. You hear the captain go in. You hear her sort, by the door, in her flat voice, by what is in their hands, and it is as rough as a road. The men go to one side and the rest to another, and a number of the men who had no jar in their hands are among the men, because they are grown, and it is a thing that is easier to say than to unsay. You stand under the pines and let it be done, and it is a thing that you think about, for a good while, on the way back to the city, with the dust on your shoes.
  ?end
-> farm_end

# farm_sort
$ b1.farm = "sorted"
$ humanity += 1
You go to the barn door. The light is behind it, in the cracks of the boards, thin and white and steady, and you can feel it on your skin like the heat of a face that is very near to your own. You knock. It is an absurd thing, a vampire in the dark at a barn door, knocking, and it is the only thing that you can think of. The low talking stops.
"I am the one who was on the hill," you say. You say it through the boards. "I want to speak to whoever is the oldest."
It is a long time before anyone answers. Then the bar goes up, and the door opens a hand's breadth, and a very old woman is standing there in a grey shawl with a little clay lamp in her two hands, and her eyes are quite clear, and she looks up at you with a fierce, tired, unfrightened scorn.
"Strix," she says. She says it quite calmly. "Is it you that they sent?"
You say that you are not a thing that she has been told about. You say that you have come to ask her to send out every man who is holding a jar, one at a time, and to let the others go. You say that she may stay, with the rest, and that nobody will touch them if the jars are put down in the yard, in a row, in the grass. You say it as steadily as you can. It is not the thing that you would say, if you were a better liar. It is the thing that is true.
She looks at you for a very long time.
?if b1.war_terms == "mercy"
  "It is more than the last ones said," says the old woman. She does not seem to think that it is very much. "I will ask them." She turns. She says something low, in the barn, in the old Latin that the Igniferi use, and there is a kind of rustle, and a murmur, and a young voice, quite a boy's, cries out sharply and is hushed. The first man comes out. He puts down the jar in the grass without a word, and holds his hands open, and looks at the stars. Then the second. It is slow. It takes the better part of an hour. Of the eleven, six put down their jars. Five do not, and go back into the barn, and the old woman closes the door, very gently, on their faces. It is not what you hoped. It is a great deal more than you feared, and the captain, behind you, has not drawn her sword.
  $ _slain += 1
  $ _spared += 28
  $ flag b1.hill_known
  $ velati_regard += 1
?else
  "I will ask them," says the old woman, at last. It is not a promise. She turns, and she says something low, in the barn, in the old Latin that the Igniferi use, and there is a rustle, and a murmur, and then a long and dreadful silence. When she turns back, she is not looking at you. "They say that they will not put them down," she says. "They say that it is the only thing that they have. I have told them that I will not be the one to tell them otherwise." She draws herself up. "You may take the old. You may take the little ones. I will not leave."
  You get them out. It is the thing that you can do, and you do it, in a long file, in the dark, past the captain, with the old woman's hand on the doorpost and her face turned to the light. You take twenty. You take every one who is not holding a jar and will go. And then the captain walks forward, with two of hers, to the barn door, and you do not follow her. You walk the old and the small down the road to the pines, and the youngest of them, a girl, asks you in a clear voice, with great interest, whether you are going to eat them.
  $ _slain += 3
  $ _spared += 20
  $ flag b1.hill_known
  $ humanity += 1
?end
Behind you, in the barn, the white light goes out. It does not go out all at once.
<~

# farm_open
## The back door
The back of the barn is a great black wall of sagging boards, with a low door in it, barred on the outside, and an orchard beyond. You go along it in the dark, under the apple trees, with the fallen fruit going soft under your feet. You lift the bar. It is very quiet. The people inside have gone still, and you can hear thirty hearts, some of them very small, beating like moths in a lamp.
You open the door. You say, into the dark and the white light, in a low voice, that they are to go out the back, through the orchard, and run. You say that they are to run until they are out of sight of the road. You say that they are to keep going.
Nobody moves. And then a woman, a young one, with a child on her hip, goes out past you into the apple trees without a word. She does not look at you. She is gone. Another goes. And another, and the old, and the small, in a long grey rustle, like a flock of birds going up, and the men with the jars go last, very slowly, with their lamps in front of them and their eyes on you, and the white light moves through the orchard like a drowned moon.
Behind you, at the front of the barn, you hear the captain say, in a voice of ice, "What is the meaning of this?"
She comes round the end of the barn with four of hers, quite fast, and she stops. She has seen the door. She looks at the orchard, where the last of the grey cloaks is going over the wall, and she looks at you.
"I will give you the choice," says the captain, quite softly, "of the only two things that I can think of."
?if b1.war_terms == "total" or b1.cell == "theron"
  -> farm_fight
?else
  The captain looks at the orchard for a long moment. She sighs, and puts her blade away. "It is not what the council said," she says, "and it is not what I would have done. But it is a good deal nearer what the council said than the other." She has a small bitter look. "I will say that I could not find the door. It is a most unfortunate thing, in the dark." She turns and goes back round the barn, and her people go after her, and you stand under the apple trees by yourself, with the white lamplight going away across the fields.
  $ _spared += 20
  $ _slain += 1
?end
$ flag b1.hill_known
