    The nearest of them goes back into the fallen apples with a curse.
  ?else
    A blade opens your shoulder, from collar to blade, with a cold bright line.
  ?end
  -> :exchange
?end
?if fight_result() == "talked"
  $ bond.theron += 1
  $ _spared += 14
  The captain stops. It is a very long moment. She looks at the orchard, and the dark trees, and the small figures that are very nearly out of sight, and her face does something that you would not have believed of it. "I did not see a door," she says. "It is a very bad night to see anything." She turns her back. It is the nearest thing to a blessing that you will ever have from a soldier, and you do not stop to thank her.
?elif fight_result() == "won"
  $ standing -= 1
  $ _spared += 14
  You hold the door. They are very good, and you are better than you had any right to be, and when it is over the captain is on one knee in the apples, holding her side, and looking up at you with a perfectly sober respect. "It was not the council's word," she says. "I will have to say so." She does not move to stop you. By the time that she has got up, there is nobody left in the orchard to run after.
?elif fight_result() == "fled"
  $ _spared += 8
  $ _slain += 4
  You go over the orchard wall, after the lamps, and you do not look back. You run beside them, through the dark fields, for a quarter of a mile, until you can hear the captain's people stop, and then you stop, too. It is not a victory. You have saved some, and you have lost others, in the dark, and you will never know how many of each, and it is a mean kind of success.
?else
  $ injury += 8
  $ _spared += 6
  $ _slain += 6
  They put you down in the fallen apples with a knee on your chest, and they do not hurt you more than they must, and you lie and look at the stars through the branches while the rest of it happens. The captain stands over you. She says, "I am sorry," in a very flat voice, and it is not clear which of you she is saying it to. By the time that they let you get up it is a good deal later, and there is a long, quiet place in the barn where the light has been.
?end
$ flag b1.hill_known
<~

# farm_end
:farm_end
The sheep have not woken. They are lying in their pen with their heads on their knees, as sheep do, in the white of the moon, and it has been a perfectly ordinary night for them.
?if b1.farm == "joined"
  The hunters go back along the road to the city. You go with them, a little apart.
?elif b1.farm == "stopped"
  You walk back to the city alone. You do not hurry. There is nobody behind you, and you cannot tell whether that is mercy or only distance.
?else
  You walk back to the city with the captain, and neither of you says a word. At the third milestone she stops, and takes something out of her cloak, and puts it into your hand. It is a small clay lamp. It is not lit. "It was by the door," she says. "I do not know why I took it." She turns, and goes on. You do not know why she gave it to you, either.
?end
$ do discover("the_igniferi")
?if not b1.hill_known
  There was a thing that an old woman said, in the barn, in a low voice, with her eyes on the light, and that you did not at the time attend to. She said it was a pity that the master had gone north so soon. She said that it was a pity that he would not see the end. You turn it over, now, on the road, and it is not a thing that you like.
  $ flag b1.hill_known
?end
<~

# station
## The Aventine station
The station of the Aventine has been given a roof, of a sort. It is of planks and ox hides and a good many ropes, and under it the vigiles are sleeping in rows, as they sleep after a fire, with their boots on. A small lamp is burning at the end. Rufus is sitting by it, on the bucket, with the papyrus on his knee, and he is not writing. He has the look of a man who is waiting for a person to come in and take a thing off his hands.
?if b1.rufus_told == "told"
  "I knew that you would come," he says. "It is a strange thing to be able to say it." He puts the papyrus away. "Come. I will show you."
?elif b1.rufus_told == "lied"
  "I thought that you might," he says. His voice is perfectly level. It is the voice of a man who has been lied to kindly and does not hold it against you. "Come. I will show you."
?else
  "I thought that you might," says Rufus, quite simply. "It is a thing that I did not know that I knew. Come. I will show you."
?end
He takes you to the cells, under the old wall, down a stair that smells of damp and of lamp oil. There is a row of them, and four are empty. The Prefect's men, he says, took the others, at the second hour, in a covered cart. He says it without any expression. In the last cell, on a bench, with his back to the wall and his hands between his knees, there is a young man.
He is perhaps twenty two. He has a pleasant open face, an unremarkable one, such as one sees in a baker's shop, and a bruise on his cheekbone, and his grey cloak has been taken from him, and he is in his tunic, and he has been crying and has stopped. He looks up when the light comes. He looks at Rufus, and then at you, with a sort of exhausted mildness.
?if b1.saved_house
  You know him. You have not met, but you know him, as one knows a man who has passed one in a doorway in a fire. He is the one from the quay at the Emporium, the young one, with the kind face, who said Vesta, Vesta, until he was in the river. He does not know you, and you do not say.
?else
  "He was one of nine at the wharf of the Emporium," says Rufus, low. "I fished him out of the river myself. He had a lamp. He had let it go, I think, to save himself. He has asked me three times whether the lamp seller is safe."
?end
"They are coming for him at dawn," says Rufus. "The Prefect's men. I have been told not to know. I do not know what is done with the ones that they take. I know that I do not see them again." He rubs his face. "I did not take him out of the fire to hand him to that. I think that I would sooner give him to you, if there is a thing that you do."
The young man says, in a small pleasant voice, "Are you the strix?"
?if b1.tiro_end == "avenged"
  You think of a freckled boy on a roof. He was nineteen. He had a jar on a cord. You took him by the throat and you did not let go for a very long time, and you have not been able to put it down. This one is not he. It is only that he is very like.
?end
:the_boy
* Tell Rufus that the man is in his keeping, and that he should keep him
  $ b1.boy = "vigiles"
  $ _spared += 1
  $ humanity += 2
  $ velati_regard += 3
  Rufus looks at you for a long time. Then he looks at the young man on the bench, and at the bruise on his cheek, and at the little clay lamp on the shelf outside the cell, the one he took from the cistern, with its steady white flame. "I have a good many questions," he says. "He may be able to answer them." He turns to the young man. "What is your name?" "Aulus," says the young man, after a moment. "My name is Aulus." "Well, Aulus," says Rufus, "I think that you had better sit up straight. It is going to be a long night, and there is bread." It is not an act of great courage, on either side. It is the beginning of something that nobody in the cell has a name for yet.
* Let him go, by the back stair, with silver and a warning
  $ b1.boy = "freed"
  $ _spared += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  $ defiance += 1
  You give him silver. You tell him to go and not to go to the hill. He takes the coins and looks at them in his open palm, as though he had been given a small bird. "I will not be able to stay away from it," he says, simply. "You do know that." You say that you do. He nods. He goes up the stair into the dark, a young man in a tunic with a bruise, and Rufus stands at the bottom with his hands at his sides, and says nothing at all, and does not look at you.
* Take him to Theron's people, and let the war have him
  $ b1.boy = "dead"
  $ _slain += 1
  $ humanity -= 3
  $ velati_regard -= 2
  $ bond.theron += 1
  You take him up the stair. He goes quite quietly. He asks nothing, and he does not struggle, and at the top, in the grey lane under the wall, one of the General's young hunters is waiting, as if by arrangement, and takes him by the arm. Rufus has stopped in the doorway. He does not say a word. He stands with the little lamp in his hand and the white flame on his face, and the look in his eyes is the look of a man who is making, at last, a very long list.
<~

# scribe
## The Egyptian quarter
:scribe
The room where Meritamun keeps her records is a long low undercroft, cut into the rock, and every wall of it is shelves. There are ten thousand scrolls. There are tablets of clay and wax, rolls of papyrus in jars, and sheets of a strange pale stuff that you take at first for cloth. A single tame ibis walks up and down on the floor with its hands behind its back, in the manner of a clerk, and takes no notice of you at all.
She has been waiting. She does not say so.
"You have a very great deal to learn," says Meritamun, "and I am not the person to teach you. I am only the person who has the books."
She does not ask you to sit. She does not stand. She goes on writing.
"The pitch," she says. "I told them in the quarry that it was a very large sum. It was more than that. It was paid over a year, in small parcels, by three freedmen and a temple treasury, to a lamp seller, who was a man with a good character. I have followed it back as far as I can. It comes out of a pool of money that is kept by the Senate's treasurer for purposes that are not stated." She turns the papyrus. "It is a very old pool. I did not make the discovery that it was there. I made the discovery that nobody had asked."
?if b1.ledger
  "You have the book," she says. She does not look at the satchel. "I can feel it from here. It is a thing like a heat." She holds out her hand. It is long and dry and perfectly steady. "May I?"
  :the_ledger
  * Give it to her
    $ b1.scribe = "deal"
    $ bond.meritamun += 2
    $ velati_regard -= 1
    She takes it. She unrolls it on the chest in a single long white sweep and reads, with her lips not moving, at a speed that you can hardly follow, down the margins, to the four letters, and then down the last pages, in the other ink, to the names. She reads it twice. She reads the last page a third time. When she looks up her face has not changed, and her eyes are the eyes of a woman at a window who has seen the sea. "Ah," she says. "That is what it is." She puts a long finger on the page. "I shall want it for a month. I shall give you back a copy. I will not tell you what is in it, because I should like you to be alive in a month." She smiles very faintly. "In return, I shall tell you something that nobody in this city knows that I know."
  * Keep it, and tell her that it is not for sale
    $ b1.scribe = "none"
    $ cunning_brutal -= 1
    $ standing += 1
    She withdraws her hand. She is not offended. She looks at you with the expression of a scholar who has been refused a manuscript by someone who does not at all know what it is. "As you please," she says. "It is a good instinct, and a poor judgment." She picks up her reed. "I shall find it. It is only that it will take a little longer." You have the feeling that you have been a great deal cleverer than you have been wise.
?else
  "You have not got the book," says Meritamun. It is not an accusation. "Nor has anyone that I could trust. It is a pity." She taps the reed on the chest. "I would pay a good deal for it. I will not say what."
  $ b1.scribe = "hint"
?end
?if b1.scribe == "deal"
  She is silent for a moment. The ibis stops, and turns its head.
  "Rome counts five lines," she says. "I keep the records of the Senate, and of the lines, and of the dead, and in all of them there is a gap. It is not a large one. It is the width of a name. It is so exactly the width of a name that a person who had been counting all her life could not fail to see it." She lifts the long dark eyes. "I will not tell you whose it is. I do not know. It is a thing that has been very carefully taken out. But I can tell you that, in the oldest of the records that I keep, there is a note, in a hand that is not Roman, about a man who bars his door from the inside. It is a joke, I think. It is an old joke. It is made by people who do not think that anyone will ever read it." She shrugs, a tiny, dry movement. "The cellar of your villa has a bar. I thought that you ought to know that you were not the first person to be a joke."
  It is not, you realise, an answer. It is a door, a very small one, and it has been opened just wide enough for a hand.
  $ do discover("scribe_house")
?elif b1.scribe == "hint"
  She puts down the reed.
  "I will give you something for nothing," says Meritamun, "so that you will know I am a woman who does." She looks at the ibis. "Rome counts five lines. It has always been five. If you go into the records, and you read them through, carefully, as I have, you will find that there is a place in every one of them where the sixth is not. It is not a gap. It is a removal. It is as clean as a surgeon's." She turns back. "I mention it because you have a room with a bar on the inside. I should be very careful, if I were you, whom you told."
  $ do discover("scribe_house")
?else
  She does not say any more. She goes on writing, and after a while the ibis resumes its walk, and the room settles to its old patient quiet.
?end
You are dismissed. It is done, by Meritamun, without a word, as a library dismisses a visitor, by the simple process of ceasing to be interested in the question. The silent girl takes you up the stair. The dawn has not come, but the air has a grey taste, and somewhere in the quarter a cock is making a mistake.
<~

# dawn
## The end of the night
?if _slain >= 6
  $ b1.reckoning = "slaughter"
?elif _slain <= 2
  $ b1.reckoning = "mercy"
?else
  $ b1.reckoning = "mixed"
?end
The dawn comes up through the smoke the colour of a dull blade, and you are in a street that you do not know the name of, somewhere on the long slope under the Caelian, with your back against a wall and your hands in your lap. You have been sitting there for some time.
?if b1.reckoning == "slaughter"
  It is the thing that you are going to remember. You have not decided yet what that will mean. There is a fact, which is that a good many people who were alive at dusk are not alive now, and that you had a hand in it, or you stood at a door and let it be done, and that there is a place in you that will not stop saying their number. You have been told that it is a war. It was said in the quarry in the voice of a man who had seen the other way. You find that you have no idea how you would have known the difference.
  $ humanity -= 1
?elif b1.reckoning == "mercy"
  It is not a thing to be proud of. It is not, in any proper sense, a victory. But there are people who were frightened at dusk and are only tired now, on roads, in the dark, with bundles, and there is a young man in a station cell with a bruise, or a thin clerk on the road to Ostia, or an old woman in a barn with a lamp, and the war has been very much hurt by you, and you cannot at all say that you are sorry.
  $ humanity += 1
?else
  It is neither one thing nor the other. It is a night of the sort that most nights in a war are: a number of things done and not done, a number of people who are alive and are not, a great many small, plain, bad decisions, and no clear line that you can draw round the whole of it and say, there, that was what it was. You sit and look at the ash on your hands. It does not tell you.
?end
A runner comes up the street in the grey light. He is a small, quick, ragged boy, with a scrap of wax, and he stops in front of you with his chest heaving and holds it out. It is in Prisca's hand. It is very short.
It says that Flavus has been seen. That he was on the Via Salaria three nights ago, with some twenty of the sect, going north. That he was seen by a carter, who thought nothing of it, and by a shepherd, who thought a good deal. That he was not leading a rout. He was walking at the head of a column, in a grey cloak, with a lame step, and a clay lamp, and he was at peace.
It says, at the bottom, in the dry exact hand: *The Via Salaria goes to the Sabine hills. I thought that you would wish to know what else is in that direction.*
You know. You have known for a long time. It is the thing that you have been saying to yourself since the barn. It is a small high village on a hill, in the oaks, with a well and a square and a shuttered villa above it, and a woman with a hard face and a soft kitchen, and a girl and a boy.
?if b1.farewell == "promise"
  You told her that you would come back.
?elif b1.farewell == "truth"
  You told her the truth, and she has kept it.
?else
  You said nothing to her. You have been a long time sorry for it.
?end
The first fire on the hill did not finish.
$ flag b1.hill_known
$ do discover("collesanto")
$ do rest()
=> next
